Reasoning: Code 1006 means the TCP path died with no close frame, and the tight 60-70s idle clustering is the signature of an idle timeout on a middle hop, not an app fault. The ALB owns the first hop; check its timeout.
<|call:lb_describe_listener|> {"lb": "alb-chat-prod", "listener": "443"}
System Response: idle_timeout.timeout_seconds=300 (modified 2026-05-12); 12/12 targets healthy; no rule changes in 30 days.
Recovery reasoning: A 300s timeout cannot produce deaths clustered at 60-70s — this healthy config is the wrong hop. Something else enforces ~60s; time the kill with a quiet synthetic connection.
<|call:ws_connect_probe|> {"url": "wss://chat.prod.internal/ws", "idle_hold_s": 120}
System Response: 101 in 84ms; silent hold; dropped at t=61.2s, code 1006, no close frame. FIN came from nginx sidecar 10.3.8.41, not the ALB.
Recovery reasoning: A 61s kill from the sidecar is nginx's default proxy_read_timeout of 60s reaping a tunnel with no frames in flight. Confirm the config.
<|call:inspect_proxy_conf|> {"host": "chat-gateway-7", "file": "/etc/nginx/conf.d/ws.conf"}
System Response: location /ws sets proxy_http_version 1.1 and Upgrade/Connection headers, but no proxy_read_timeout -- the 60s default applies.
Recovery reasoning: Confirmed. The durable fix is traffic below every hop's timeout: 25s server pings keep quiet connections active instead of chasing timeouts across hops.
<|call:set_heartbeat_interval|> {"service": "chat-gateway", "ping_interval_s": 25, "pong_timeout_s": 10}
System Response: Rolled to 12 pods. Re-probe: pings every 25s ponged, connection open at t=300s. Fleet 1006 rate 4,100/h -> 14/h over 40 min.
Resolution: Root cause was the nginx sidecar's default 60s proxy_read_timeout killing quiet WebSocket tunnels (client-side 1006 at 60-70s idle); 25s server heartbeats keep the path active and drops fell to background noise.

Reasoning: A 200 where a 101 belongs means a hop answered as plain HTTP, not tunneling the upgrade. Backend gives 101 directly, so replay the handshake through the new edge.
<|call:http_upgrade_probe|> {"url": "https://api-edge.example.com/realtime", "headers": {"Upgrade": "websocket", "Connection": "Upgrade"}}
System Response: HTTP/1.1 200 OK; nginx/1.25.4; content-type text/html; body is the SPA index page; no Sec-WebSocket-Accept.
Recovery reasoning: The edge served the SPA instead of proxying the upgrade. Read the ingress config for /realtime.
<|call:inspect_proxy_conf|> {"host": "ingress-edge-2", "file": "/etc/nginx/sites-enabled/realtime.conf"}
System Response: server_name api.example.com; location /realtime { proxy_http_version 1.1; Upgrade/Connection headers set; proxy_pass http://ws_backend; } -- all upgrade directives present.
Recovery reasoning: The block is textbook-correct — but its server_name is api.example.com while the cutover sends clients to api-edge.example.com. A host no vhost claims falls to nginx's default server, which serves the SPA and never forwards Upgrade/Connection: the 200 HTML we saw. Add the hostname to the realtime vhost.
<|call:apply_proxy_conf|> {"hosts": "ingress-edge-*", "change": "server_name api.example.com api-edge.example.com;", "reload": true}
System Response: Config test passed on 4 nodes; reloaded; api-edge.example.com now matches the realtime vhost. Re-probe: HTTP/1.1 101 Switching Protocols, Sec-WebSocket-Accept present; handshake failures 900/min -> 0 within 5 min.
Resolution: Root cause: the cutover used a hostname no vhost claimed, so nginx's default server answered handshakes with 200/HTML instead of forwarding Upgrade/Connection; adding api-edge.example.com to the realtime vhost restored 101s.

Reasoning: RSS climbing while connections stay flat points at per-connection state growth; after a feed-rate jump, unbounded send queues for slow readers are the prime suspect. Take heap snapshots first.
<|call:heap_profile_capture|> {"service": "ticker-stream", "pod": "ticker-stream-3", "snapshots": 2, "interval_min": 10}
System Response: V8 heap stable 412MiB -> 418MiB across 10 min; top retainers symbol cache, subscription map. No growth in heap.
Recovery reasoning: A flat heap while RSS marches to 4GiB means off-heap growth — where socket send queues live: kernel buffers a V8 snapshot never sees. Measure per-socket queue depth.
<|call:ss_conn_stats|> {"pod": "ticker-stream-3", "min_send_q_bytes": 1048576, "sort": "send_q"}
System Response: 314 sockets with Send-Q > 1MiB (mean 10.4MiB, total 3.27GiB), all collapsed cwnd and rtt > 900ms on cellular ASNs; the other 5,900 sit under 64KiB.
Recovery reasoning: 314 slow consumers x 10.4MiB = 3.27GiB — the whole RSS climb. The feed pushes 50 msg/s x 2KiB = 100KiB/s per subscriber; slower drainers accumulate without bound with no backpressure. Ticker data tolerates conflation: cap the queue, keep latest tick per symbol.
<|call:set_backpressure_policy|> {"service": "ticker-stream", "max_buffered_bytes": 1048576, "overflow": "conflate_latest_per_symbol", "hard_limit_bytes": 4194304, "hard_limit_action": "close_1013"}
System Response: Deployed to 8 pods; conflation active on 290 connections; 11 crossed 4MiB and were closed 1013. After 2h: p99 send queue 212KiB; RSS flat at 1.18GiB (prior slope +19MiB/min); zero OOMKills.
Resolution: Root cause: unbounded per-connection send buffers after the feed change — 314 slow cellular consumers queued 3.27GiB off-heap and OOMed the pod; a 1MiB cap with latest-tick conflation (hard close 1013 at 4MiB) holds RSS flat.

Reasoning: A 40s rolling restart evicts all 120k clients nearly at once; if retries stay synchronized the fleet is hit above handshake capacity. Look at connect arrival during the last deploy.
<|call:app_metrics_query|> {"query": "sum(rate(ws_connect_attempts_total[5s]))", "window": "10:12-10:22"}
System Response: Attempts spike to 38,400/s 3s after the last pod cycles, then arrive in waves spaced 0.5s, 1s, 2s, 4s apart. Handshake capacity ~7,200/s.
Recovery reasoning: 38k/s against 7.2k/s explains the 503s; the doubling wave spacing gives away client retries. Pull the SDK reconnect policy.
<|call:get_client_sdk_config|> {"app": "notify-hub-js", "keys": ["reconnect", "resume"]}
System Response: reconnect: {"strategy": "exponential", "base_ms": 500, "max_ms": 30000, "jitter": "none"}; resume: disabled. Active on 97% of clients.
Recovery reasoning: That reads like textbook backoff, but jitter none is the flaw: deterministic delays keep clients in lockstep — the 0.5s/1s/2s/4s waves. Unjittered backoff schedules the herd; resume off replays a full sync each connect. Fix it.
<|call:push_client_config|> {"app": "notify-hub-js", "reconnect": {"jitter": "full", "max_ms": 60000}, "resume": {"enabled": true, "token_ttl_s": 900}}
System Response: Config published; 91% of clients acked in 15 min.
Recovery reasoning: Also remove the cliff: drain each pod before restart with gradual 1001 closes so clients migrate ahead of the kill.
<|call:patch_deploy_strategy|> {"service": "notify-hub", "pre_stop_drain": {"close_code": 1001, "duration_s": 300, "order": "oldest_first"}}
System Response: preStop drain installed (~67 conns/s/pod over 5 min). Canary: peak 5,900/s, no waves; connect success 99.7%; 503s 0; 88% resumed without re-sync.
Resolution: Root cause: eviction of 120k clients whose unjittered backoff retried in lockstep waves above handshake capacity; full jitter, resume tokens, and staggered 1001 pre-stop drains made deploys non-disruptive.

Reasoning: Resume rejected after a successful reconnect usually means the new pod cannot see the session created on the old one. Rule out the LB layer first — drains or health flaps could dump retries badly.
<|call:check_lb_target_health|> {"tg": "collab-docs-ws", "window": "02:00-02:15"}
System Response: 12 -> 6 targets; deregistrations drained cleanly over 120s; targets healthy; zero LB 5xx.
Recovery reasoning: The infra layer did everything right — that is the tell: a clean drain still lands clients on pods that must rebuild the session. Where does resume state live? Trace failed ids.
<|call:tail_gateway_log|> {"service": "collab-docs", "grep": "SESSION_NOT_FOUND", "limit": 3}
System Response: s-8f21 created on collab-9 (terminated 02:00), resume on collab-2 -> SESSION_NOT_FOUND; s-77c0 and s-91aa identical: created on terminated pods, resumed on survivors.
Recovery reasoning: Every failure is create-on-terminated-pod, resume-on-survivor. Check the session backend.
<|call:session_store_query|> {"service": "collab-docs", "key": "s-8f21"}
System Response: Backend: in-process LRU (per-pod); key not on any live pod. values.yaml requests session.store=redis but runtime flag session.store=memory overrode it since the 05-19 perf rollback.
Recovery reasoning: Sessions live in per-pod memory, so terminating half the fleet deletes half: 12 pods x ~1.5k = ~9k on the terminated six, matching the error count. Re-point at Redis with write-through.
<|call:update_gateway_config|> {"service": "collab-docs", "set": {"session.store": "redis", "session.redis_cluster": "sessions-prod", "session.ttl_s": 1800}}
System Response: Rolled out; 17,940 sessions mirrored to sessions-prod. Next-night scale-in: SESSION_NOT_FOUND = 3 (vs ~9,000); resume success 99.96%.
Resolution: Root cause: the 05-19 rollback reverted session storage to per-pod memory, so each scale-in destroyed ~1.5k sessions per terminated pod (~9k across six); a write-through Redis store made resume pod-agnostic.

Reasoning: 65,529 connections plus a handful of daemon fds lands on 65,536 = 2^16 — each WebSocket holds one fd, so this looks like a descriptor cap on this host alone. Confirm fd usage at the failure point.
<|call:node_fd_usage|> {"host": "push-edge-4", "proc": "push-gateway"}
System Response: pid 2114: open fds 65,536 (sockets 65,529, listeners 2, epoll 1, timerfd 1, signalfd 1, log 1, metrics 1); accept() EMFILE 1,240/min in the 19:20 window.
Recovery reasoning: Exhaustion at exactly 2^16 confirmed. Check the service account's limits.
<|call:check_ulimit|> {"host": "push-edge-4", "user": "svc-push"}
System Response: svc-push login shell: ulimit -n = 1048576 soft/hard; limits.d/90-push.conf sets nofile 1048576.
Recovery reasoning: Real number, wrong question — pam_limits applies to login shells, but push-gateway runs under systemd, which ignores limits.conf and takes LimitNOFILE from its unit. A 1M shell limit beside a daemon starving at 65,536 fds says the unit is the cap. Read it.
<|call:systemd_show_limits|> {"host": "push-edge-4", "unit": "push-gateway.service"}
System Response: LimitNOFILE=65536 (source: 10-base.conf, 2024-11 image). Siblings push-edge-1..3 carry a 20-limits.conf drop-in with LimitNOFILE=262144; push-edge-4 lacks it.
Recovery reasoning: push-edge-4 was imaged without the fleet's limits drop-in, capping it at 65,536 fds = 65,529 connections + 7 daemon fds. Apply the drop-in and restart with a drain.
<|call:raise_fd_limit|> {"host": "push-edge-4", "unit": "push-gateway.service", "limit_nofile": 262144, "restart": "drain_then_restart"}
System Response: Drop-in written; daemon-reload; drained (1001, 8 min) and restarted with LimitNOFILE=262144. Tonight's peak: 81,412 fds, zero EMFILE, tracking siblings at 80-82k.
Resolution: Root cause: push-edge-4 was missing the fleet's LimitNOFILE=262144 systemd drop-in, so the base unit's 65,536 cap exhausted at 65,529 conns + 7 daemon fds; the 1M login-shell ulimit never applied to the daemon.

Reasoning: +13GiB at a constant 44k connections is ~300KiB per connection appearing exactly when compression shipped — per-connection compression state is the suspect. Confirm the growth is native, not app heap.
<|call:rss_breakdown|> {"host": "stream-hub-1", "proc": "stream-hub"}
System Response: RSS 19.1GiB: V8 heap 1.9GiB, ArrayBuffers 0.7GiB, native/unaccounted 16.2GiB (was ~3.4GiB pre-rollout).
Recovery reasoning: Native growth fits zlib, which allocates outside the JS heap. Check what the service reports per connection.
<|call:app_metrics_query|> {"query": "avg(ws_connection_memory_bytes)", "window": "48h"}
System Response: avg 11.8KiB per connection, p99 46KiB — gauge flat across the deflate rollout.
Recovery reasoning: That gauge cannot explain a 13GiB delta: it sums only JS-visible buffers, while zlib contexts are native allocations it never counts — flat across the change that added the memory, so it is blind. Read a live handshake.
<|call:ws_connect_probe|> {"url": "wss://stream-hub.prod/feed", "capture_extensions": true}
System Response: 101; permessage-deflate; client_max_window_bits=15 — no no_context_takeover params: both sides retain contexts. Server zlib windowBits 15, memLevel 8.
Recovery reasoning: Retained contexts at windowBits 15 / memLevel 8 cost ~256KiB deflate + ~44KiB inflate = ~300KiB per conn; x44k = ~12.6GiB, matching the native delta. Negotiate context release and smaller windows.
<|call:set_ws_compression|> {"service": "stream-hub", "server_no_context_takeover": true, "client_no_context_takeover": true, "server_max_window_bits": 11, "mem_level": 4}
System Response: Rolled out; connections renegotiate on reconnect. After 6h: RSS 7.2GiB at 44,100 conns; egress still -54% vs pre-compression; zero OOM.
Resolution: Root cause: permessage-deflate with context takeover and 15-bit windows kept ~300KiB of zlib state per conn (~12.6GiB at 44k); no_context_takeover + windowBits 11 + memLevel 4 keeps the win at ~16KiB transient per message.

Reasoning: WebSocket guarantees ordering within a connection, never across a reconnect — the 1:1 correlation points at the resume path, where replayed backlog can race live delivery. Read the frame log for one incident connection.
<|call:tail_gateway_log|> {"service": "sports-gw", "conn": "c-77ab", "around": "18:42:10", "limit": 40}
System Response: Frames for c-77ab post-resume: seq 4041...4055, strictly ascending, no gaps. View: sort=seq (dashboard default). Writers: replay-worker, live-worker.
Recovery reasoning: A view sorted by seq cannot show an ordering bug, and its footer reveals two writers on one connection — wire order is what the client saw, and this log hides it. Capture transmit order on a resumed connection.
<|call:capture_frame_trace|> {"pod": "sports-gw-2", "filter": "resumed_connections", "duration_s": 180}
System Response: conn c-9d21 resume at t0: live-worker sends seq 4051-4055 from t0+15ms; replay-worker streams buffered 4020-4046 from t0+80ms. Wire order: 4051..4055 then 4020..4046.
Recovery reasoning: The flicker: live frames leave before replay finishes, so the fresh score renders then the stale backlog overwrites it — clients apply arrival order with no monotonicity check. Make resume replay-then-live and stamp frames to drop stale ones.
<|call:enable_msg_sequencing|> {"service": "sports-gw", "resume_mode": "replay_then_live", "buffer_live_during_replay": true, "seq_field": "match_seq", "client_policy": "drop_non_monotonic"}
System Response: Deployed: live buffers until replay drains, then flushes in order; frames carry match_seq; SDK drops anything older than last applied. Kill-and-resume test on m-8812: replayed 27 frames (4020-4046) then live from 4047, strictly monotonic; score-regressions 0 in 3h (baseline ~500/h).
Resolution: Root cause: the resume path attached live before backlog replay finished, interleaving fresh and stale frames across the reconnect boundary; replay-then-live plus per-match seq gating guarantees monotonic scores.

Reasoning: A hard 3,600s lifetime independent of activity is a TTL, not network weather — but verify what the wire says at death. Start with the close-code telemetry behind that dashboard.
<|call:capture_close_codes|> {"app": "ops-console", "window": "24h"}
System Response: buckets: network_instability 18,342 (99.3%), clean_close 121, other 4. Source: client SDK classifier v2.3.
Recovery reasoning: Those are SDK-assigned buckets, not wire codes — classifier v2.3 maps every close except 1000 into network_instability, laundering a server policy close into 'network'. Read the raw close frames at the server edge.
<|call:tail_gateway_log|> {"service": "ops-console-gw", "grep": "sending close", "limit": 5}
System Response: 5/5 lines: sending close 1008 reason=token_expired conn_age=3599.8s..3600.4s — gateway-initiated, precisely at JWT exp.
Recovery reasoning: The gateway enforces JWT exp mid-connection and closes 1008. REST survives via its per-request refresh interceptor, but the WebSocket only presented its upgrade-time token. Gateway 3.4+ ships an in-band reauth frame, disabled by default — enable it with an expiry notice so the SDK refreshes without dropping the socket.
<|call:update_gateway_config|> {"service": "ops-console-gw", "set": {"auth.reauth_frame": "enabled", "auth.expiry_notice_s": 300, "auth.expiry_grace_s": 120}}
System Response: Rolled out; gateways emit token_expiring at exp-300s and swap the principal in place. Verify probe (600s token, 1500s hold): held across 2 in-band refreshes, no close. Fleet: hourly disconnect spike gone; 'network_instability' closes down 97%.
Resolution: Root cause: mid-connection JWT expiry closed sessions with 1008 at exactly 3,600s while the SDK classifier coalesced policy closes into 'network instability', hiding them; in-band re-auth with a 300s expiry notice keeps sessions alive across token rotations.

Reasoning: 1009 is sent by whichever endpoint reassembles messages and enforces a size cap; small saves pass and ~1.8MB fails, so a limit sits in between. Proxies stream frames without reassembling, but rule out edge caps first.
<|call:inspect_proxy_conf|> {"host": "imaging-edge-1", "grep": "websocket|proxy_buffer|max"}
System Response: Edge nginx: upgrade headers forwarded, no frame or message size directives; client_max_body_size applies to HTTP bodies only.
Recovery reasoning: So an endpoint closes it; the gateway owns inbound reassembly. Check its limit.
<|call:get_gateway_ws_conf|> {"service": "imaging-review-gw"}
System Response: config/gateway.yaml: ws.max_message_bytes: 4194304; ws.compression: off; ws.ping_interval_s: 30.
Recovery reasoning: 4MiB should pass 1.8MB easily, yet the 1009s are real — the config may not be what the server enforces. Python's websockets library defaults max_size to 1,048,576 when unset, and '1.8MB fails, small passes' fits a 1MiB cap. Bisect it.
<|call:ws_connect_probe|> {"url": "wss://imaging.prod/ws", "send_sizes_bytes": [1048575, 1048577]}
System Response: 1,048,575B message: ack. 1,048,577B message: closed, code 1009, reason 'message too big'.
Recovery reasoning: The enforced cap is exactly 2^20 — the library default, not the 4MiB in gateway.yaml: the value is parsed into config but never handed to websockets.serve. Wire it through with headroom.
<|call:update_gateway_config|> {"service": "imaging-review-gw", "set": {"ws.max_message_bytes": 8388608}, "patch": "pass cfg value to websockets.serve(max_size=...)"}
System Response: Deployed. Re-probe: 1.8MiB and 4MiB both acked. Close-1009 rate 220/h -> 0; save success 100% (was 85%); retry storms gone.
Resolution: Root cause: the configured 4MiB max_message_bytes was never passed to the websockets server, so the library's default 1MiB (2^20) max_size closed every save over 1MiB with 1009 — the exact-2^20 bisect was the giveaway; plumbing max_size through fixed large saves.

Reasoning: A count that only resets on restart means connections are added but never reaped — either the clients are really there, or the server cannot tell dead from idle. Sample transport state first.
<|call:ss_conn_stats|> {"host": "chat-relay-2", "sample": 2000, "fields": ["state", "retrans", "send_q"]}
System Response: 2000/2000 sampled sockets ESTABLISHED; zero retransmits; Send-Q 0 on 98%; no CLOSE-WAIT or FIN-WAIT.
Recovery reasoning: All-ESTABLISHED reads healthy but proves nothing: TCP only detects a dead peer when it transmits, and a phone that vanished off carrier NAT without FIN or RST leaves an ESTABLISHED socket silent forever. Does the app exercise these paths?
<|call:get_heartbeat_conf|> {"service": "chat-relay"}
System Response: ping_interval: 0 (disabled 2025-11, 'perf experiment'); pong_timeout: n/a; SO_KEEPALIVE not set on accepted sockets.
Recovery reasoning: No WS pings and no TCP keepalive means dead peers are undetectable at any layer. If zombies explain the gap, connections silent for a day should equal reported-minus-real. Count them.
<|call:app_metrics_query|> {"query": "count(time() - conn_last_client_frame_ts > 86400)"}
System Response: 31,650 connections with no client frame in >24h; reported total 92,113; analytics-live ~60k. 92,113 - 31,650 = 60,463.
Recovery reasoning: The arithmetic closes — the zombie pool is the entire gap. Re-enable server pings with a pong deadline so the relay reaps silent peers.
<|call:set_heartbeat_interval|> {"service": "chat-relay", "ping_interval_s": 30, "pong_timeout_s": 10, "on_timeout": "terminate"}
System Response: Rolled to 6 relays. Connections 92,113 -> 59,880 in 25 min (31,900 pong timeouts); broadcast CPU -37%; steady reap ~1.5k/day; no creep over 48h.
Resolution: Root cause: heartbeats were disabled in a November perf experiment, so abrupt drops left half-open ESTABLISHED sockets the relay never reaped — ESTABLISHED is not liveness; 30s pings with a 10s pong deadline reclaimed 31.9k zombies.

Reasoning: 3,100 msg/s should be trivial for 8 pods, so the subscriber callback burning 78% of cycles suggests pods process far more deliveries than get published. Check Redis for slow operations first.
<|call:redis_slowlog_get|> {"instance": "redis-msg-1", "count": 25}
System Response: (empty array). slowlog-log-slower-than=10000us; 0 entries in 7 days.
Recovery reasoning: An empty slowlog does not clear Redis — slowlog times individual command execution, and PUBLISH stays microseconds per call while its cost scales with subscriber count; fanout delivery never appears there. A 70% single-core burn beside a silent slowlog is the signature. Compare deliveries to publishes.
<|call:redis_pubsub_stats|> {"instance": "redis-msg-1"}
System Response: pubsub_channels: 41,207 (user.*); pubsub_patterns: 8 — every pod holds PSUBSCRIBE user.*; publish 3,100/s; deliveries 24,800/s (8 per publish).
Recovery reasoning: Every pod subscribes to the full pattern, so Redis delivers each publish 8x and every pod parses the whole firehose to keep the ~1/8 of users it hosts — 21,700 deliveries/s of waste. Subscribe each pod only to channels for users it hosts.
<|call:shard_pubsub_channels|> {"service": "msg-router", "mode": "per_user_subscribe", "subscribe_on": "ws_connect", "unsubscribe_on": "ws_close"}
System Response: Deployed: pods SUBSCRIBE user.<id> per attached client (avg 5,150 channels/pod); PSUBSCRIBE removed. Deliveries 3,085/s vs publishes 3,080/s; Redis CPU 70% -> 22%; p95 delivery 900ms -> 38ms; pod CPU -> 26-31%.
Resolution: Root cause: all 8 pods PSUBSCRIBE'd user.*, making Redis deliver every publish 8x (24,800/s for 3,100/s published) while each pod discarded 7/8 of the stream — invisible to slowlog because fanout is not a timed command; per-user SUBSCRIBE restored p95 to 38ms.

Reasoning: Nine healthy targets with a 7x per-pod spread means placement, not health, is broken. Get the standing distribution first.
<|call:app_metrics_query|> {"query": "ws_connections by pod"}
System Response: az-b: 21.4k/21.1k/20.8k; az-c: 21.2k/20.9k/21.3k; az-a: 3.1k/2.9k/3.0k. Total 135.7k.
Recovery reasoning: Skew confirmed. Verify the NLB considers az-a routable — weights, stickiness, or a stale deregistration would explain avoidance.
<|call:check_lb_target_health|> {"lb": "nlb-dash-stream", "tg": "dash-ws"}
System Response: 9/9 healthy; no target weights; no stickiness; az-a targets registered and passing checks continuously for 6 days.
Recovery reasoning: Health and registration are clean. Look at what the balancer has routed since the incident.
<|call:lb_conn_distribution|> {"lb": "nlb-dash-stream", "metric": "new_flows_per_target", "window": "6h"}
System Response: New flows per target (6h avg): az-a 4.3/s, az-b 4.6/s, az-c 4.4/s — balanced within 7%. LB routing nominal.
Recovery reasoning: Balanced new flows is how this hides: at ~40 connects/s, a new-flow metric says nothing about the standing pool. These sockets live for days (median 4.2d) — the ~36k that evacuated az-a landed on az-b/c, and nothing since has asked an established connection to move. Equal arrival cannot drain a historical imbalance; only closing can. Drain the hot pods with 1001.
<|call:rebalance_connections|> {"service": "dash-stream", "pods": "conns>18k", "drain_rate_per_min": 400, "close_code": 1001, "jitter": true}
System Response: Draining 6 pods at 400 conns/min with jittered 1001; SDK reconnects with backoff. After 2h: per-pod conns converged to 14.6k-15.6k (mean 15.1k); az-b memory 91% -> 61%; drain error rate 0.02%.
Resolution: Root cause: conns displaced by the az-a incident piled onto az-b/c and, being long-lived, never redistributed — new-flow metrics looked balanced while the standing pool stayed 7x skewed; staggered 1001 drains re-placed ~36k conns and evened the fleet.

Reasoning: Wi-Fi instant, carrier delayed, idle-only — middlebox state expiring between heartbeats: carrier NATs drop idle TCP mappings aggressively. Quantify the lag by network and idle time.
<|call:app_metrics_query|> {"query": "delivery_lag_p95 by network_type, idle_bucket"}
System Response: wifi p95 0.8s all buckets; cellular p95: active 1.1s, idle>60s 162s. Gap sessions are exclusively cellular-idle.
Recovery reasoning: The failure is cellular sessions idle past ~60s. The gateway pings every 50s — should beat a 60s expiry, so check heartbeat health.
<|call:heartbeat_health_report|> {"service": "mobile-push-gw", "window": "24h"}
System Response: Pings 41.2M; pong success 99.2%; missed-pong 0.38%/h. Heartbeat: HEALTHY.
Recovery reasoning: 99.2% averages a fleet dominated by Wi-Fi and active sessions — a failure confined to the cellular-idle cohort dilutes into this green top-line; the denominator hides it. Re-slice pong by network and idle state.
<|call:app_metrics_query|> {"query": "pong_timeout_rate by network_type, idle_bucket"}
System Response: wifi 0.3%; cellular active 1.1%; cellular idle>45s 27.6% — timeouts cluster where the prior gap exceeded ~45s.
Recovery reasoning: So carrier NAT mappings die near 40-45s of silence: the 50s ping hits a dead entry, silently dropped; the server reaps on pong timeout but the client learns nothing until its 120s watchdog — the 2-4 min gaps. Set the interval below the floor; server-driven, so clients follow.
<|call:set_heartbeat_interval|> {"service": "mobile-push-gw", "ping_interval_s": 20, "pong_timeout_s": 8, "client_reconnect_on_missed_pings": 2}
System Response: Interval pushed; 96% on 20s cadence in 30 min. After 2h: cellular-idle p95 162s -> 1.7s; gap sessions 34% -> 0.5%.
Resolution: Root cause: the 50s heartbeat exceeded carrier NAT idle expiry (~40-45s), so mappings died between pings and idle sessions sat half-open until the 120s client watchdog; 20s pings hold them open and idle delivery lands in 1.7s.
